# Reconstructed observation summary: TinyStories-1M YPCB JTAG attempt

Capture date: 2026-08-29 (Europe/Madrid)
Board: YPCB-00338-1P1 / xc7k480tffg1156-1
Device: FTDI 0403:6014, serial 210299BF3824

## Exact primary attempt

```text
cwd=/home/roland/kev-gpt/.worktrees/kintex-selftest
nix run .#kevin-jtag -- infer --prompt "Once upon a time" --max-new-tokens 16 --timeout 300 --json-receipt /tmp/task3i-tinystories-1m-inference-receipt.json
```

During subsequent read-only USB diagnostics, a process with this exact command
line owned `/dev/bus/usb/003/063`.  After the command's configured 300-second
window, no `/tmp/task3i-tinystories-1m-inference-receipt.json` existed and the
observed process was gone.  The primary invocation's stdout, stderr, and exit
status were not captured, so this record does not assert why it ended.  No
output tokens, cycles, or board result are asserted here.

## Direct client diagnostics while the primary attempt owned the device

```text
$ /nix/store/dihyg78a9r6sdinv0wsysalmq40235mr-kevin-jtag/bin/kevin-jtag packet-selftest
JTAG_PACKET_SELFTEST_PASS cases=3

$ /nix/store/dihyg78a9r6sdinv0wsysalmq40235mr-kevin-jtag/bin/kevin-jtag infer --prompt "Once upon a time" --max-new-tokens 16 --timeout 300 --json-receipt /tmp/task3i-tinystories-1m-inference-receipt.json
RuntimeError: cannot open JTAG transport: unable to claim usb device. Make sure the default FTDI driver is not in use
```

The second command was deliberately not retried: `fuser` showed that the
primary client then owned the USB device.  This error does not establish a
kernel-driver conflict; it only establishes that the device could not be
claimed while the other client held it.

